# Force LF in the working tree for all text files. Windows-side tooling kept CRLF-ing checkouts,
# which broke real things: a guard script's shebang became "bash\r" (every hook invocation then
# failed open), and CRLF inside test raw-string literals failed two CompactFormatter tests. The
# shebang case is gone now that hooks run as `dotnet <dll> hook <name>` rather than as shell scripts,
# but C# sources and hook JSON are still load-bearing about this; the blanket rule keeps diffs clean
# everywhere else too.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.cs text eol=lf
*.csproj text eol=lf
hooks/hooks.json text eol=lf

# The one exception: cmd.exe needs CRLF. A batch file with LF line endings misparses multi-line
# constructs, so build-plugin.cmd must not be normalized with everything else.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
